# FABLE-SONNET-OWN-LOOKUP-AUDIT-20260923-131 Part 2, positive. l2_hidden_from's
# fallback ("no caller binding: use the declaration in the selected callee's
# lexical node") used to call l2_own_first(nm), which takes no callee
# parameter at all and scans l2_own_n globally for the first top-level own
# row matching nm in SOURCE order -- an unrelated, earlier-declared method's
# same-named field could win over the actual callee's own declaration.
# Fixed: l2_own_find(callee, nm), the same lookup callee's own body uses,
# reading through the ordinary own-cell emission (l2_own_ctr/l2_own_uchild)
# unchanged -- no new mechanism.
#
# Ruling (fable, the author's own norm: a local IS its canonical cell with a
# dirty load; a declaration's literal is an operator at its place, not a
# construction-time seed of the cell): the fallback reads the callee's own
# CANONICAL CELL, whatever was last published there -- not a re-extracted
# source literal. A callee that has never run has never checkpointed, so
# its cell reads 0, same as any other own field before its first publish.
#
# alpha (declared first) owns `shared`=111 -- proves the cross-method
# collision is gone (neither activation of beta ever reads 111). entry
# calls beta TWICE with no binding for `shared` anywhere: the first call
# starts beta's first activation, whose own `int: shared 222` declaration
# runs and checkpoints at return, publishing 222 to beta's own cell; the
# second call's fallback (computed BEFORE that activation starts) now
# reads that published 222. First activation sees 0 (never-run cell,
# proving no cross-contamination AND no stale seed-literal shortcut);
# second sees 222 (beta's own cell, now published) -- the discriminating
# pair no single call could prove alone.
fn: alpha () int
    int: shared 111
    return: 0
end: alpha

fn: beta () int
    c.printf: "beta sees shared=%d\n" shared
    int: shared 222
    return: 0
end: beta

alpha()
beta()
beta()
return: 0
